WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.800
Last year I did a video on cybersecurity trends,&nbsp;
and in that I talked about artificial intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.280
and I thought that was going to be important.&nbsp;
It's turned out to be very important and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:13.168
going to be even more important as we go forward.

00:00:13.168 --> 00:00:18.175
By the way, is my hair really that gray in the next deepfake? We're going to fix that. 

00:00:18.175 --> 00:00:21.351
Okay,&nbsp;let's take a look at the future. What I'm sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.520
of is that the future will look something like the&nbsp;
past. And in fact, one of the things we'll see in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:32.480
the future are more AI based threats. But more on&nbsp;
that in a few minutes. What we're going to see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.640
though, also, is that change is the only constant.&nbsp;
So that means things will be similar to the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:41.760
but there will also be new things that we're&nbsp;
going to take a look at. One of the new things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:48.080
that I think is on the positive side is that we're&nbsp;
going to see a move away from passwords toward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:54.520
past keys. There's a new standard called Fido&nbsp;
that allows you to not have to send a password,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.520 --> 00:01:00.680
but in fact, you do something that is simpler,&nbsp;
easier to use and more secure. We don't normally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:05.440
get to do both of those at the same time, and&nbsp;
we're going to need it. And what's the reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:12.080
for that? Well, because AI, as I mentioned, is&nbsp;
going to be an increasing threat factor for us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:18.160
AI based phishing emails are going to become&nbsp;
more and more common, I expect, because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:24.200
can generate what is very convincing emails to get&nbsp;
people to try to log in or share their credentials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:31.280
in ways that they shouldn't. And this is a very&nbsp;
efficient way of doing it. However, if you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:37.120
have a password in the first place to send, if you&nbsp;
only have something that is a secret that stays on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:42.040
your system, then there's no way for someone to&nbsp;
fish that out of you. So that this is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:47.160
be a good thing to try to help against that. Now,&nbsp;
there are other things that we can take a look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:54.280
that also in the air space, generative AI, I think&nbsp;
we're going to see an increased use of deepfakes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.280 --> 00:02:02.200
These are things where we simulate the voice, the&nbsp;
image, the likeness of an individual. And in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:09.080
deepfake technology has become so good and it is&nbsp;
so prevalent. In fact, if you have a mobile phone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:15.040
it's probably already built into your operating&nbsp;
system. In most cases. You may not know about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.520
but it's there. So you could use this kind of&nbsp;
technology to fake someone out, have them believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:26.480
something that's not true. For instance, have&nbsp;
someone call a relative and say, I need money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.160
It sounds like it's your voice. So they send the&nbsp;
money. So we're going to need to do more in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:35.840
of educating people about deepfakes and the threat&nbsp;
in that space, because I think we're going to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.920
more of it. And by the way, if you think deepfake&nbsp;
detection is going to be a good way to go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.480
I'm going to ask you to think again about that.&nbsp;
Deepfake technology will always keep getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:51.040
better and it will eventually be to the point&nbsp;
where I don't think detection is going to work. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.560
many cases, we've already seen this happen. So the&nbsp;
focus needs to be not on detecting the deepfake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.120
with some sort of technology, but building&nbsp;
security mechanisms around it so that we're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:07.000
reliant on the information that's in the deepfake&nbsp;
itself. Other things that we're going to take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:13.600
look at would be a threat that comes to us from&nbsp;
generative AI and that's hallucinations. And by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:17.320
the way, you didn't think I was going to actually&nbsp;
write that word out when I have a magic board that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:23.280
can autocomplete. That's what generative AI does,&nbsp;
right? So I'm leveraging that. Hallucinations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:29.240
We're going to be more and more dependent upon&nbsp;
generative AI, large language models and chat bot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:34.360
to give us information. The problem is, some of&nbsp;
the information they give us isn't always right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:39.480
And we call those hallucinations, and we're going&nbsp;
to make decisions based upon that that could cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:46.600
security threats to us. So my hope is that there&nbsp;
will be other technologies, things like retrieval,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:52.440
augmented generation, or what we call rag&nbsp;
technology that will help reinforce and make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:57.560
this system better and more accurate. Other&nbsp;
things that we can do to tune the models and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:03.560
train them better so that they don't hallucinate&nbsp;
nearly as much going forward. And then finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:07.320
I'm going to say something. I want to leave&nbsp;
you with a positive in terms of a look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:14.840
future. And that is there's this symbiotic&nbsp;
relationship between AI and cybersecurity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:22.000
and that is we're going to use AI to do a better&nbsp;
job of cybersecurity. In fact, there's a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:28.400
things that we can do in this space to leverage&nbsp;
generative AI in order to better think about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:34.080
way someone would attack us. Also summarize cases&nbsp;
and things of that sort. So I think we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:40.320
to be able to do a better job with cybersecurity&nbsp;
by leveraging AI. By the same token, we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:46.200
to need to use our cybersecurity skills in order&nbsp;
to secure this AI so that it can be trustworthy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:51.520
so that we can, in fact, believe that the&nbsp;
information it gives us is true. Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:57.680
that's the future. And it's not a big surprise&nbsp;
that the future is very heavy. However, there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:03.200
a lot of existing threats that have continued to&nbsp;
persist and will continue to persist as we move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:08.360
into the future. Let's take a quick look at the&nbsp;
scorecard from last year's predictions and see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:13.560
which ones of those actually came true and which&nbsp;
ones carry forward. I mentioned data breach last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:18.720
year when I did the video, and in fact, it turns&nbsp;
out that the cost of a data breach has continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.800
to increase. In fact, now we're on the order&nbsp;
of four and a half million dollars on average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:31.560
worldwide. And in the US that number is almost&nbsp;
twice as high. So that one I'm going to say yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:39.240
came true ransomware. In fact, we've continued to&nbsp;
see ransomware persist. The the overall numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:44.320
are a little bit down but the amount of time it&nbsp;
takes to run one of these attacks has changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:51.560
dramatically. This according to the X-Force Threat&nbsp;
Intelligence Index, which says that back in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:57.680
we were looking at 60 days on average to deploy&nbsp;
one of these. Now we're down to about four days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:04.200
So this is kind of a mixed bag. You know, this&nbsp;
is it's sort of true. Sort of not true. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:09.120
ransomware is going to continue to be a threat&nbsp;
and it's a faster threat than it used to be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:13.720
Multifactor authentication. I don't know about&nbsp;
you, but I'm definitely seeing more websites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:18.840
that are offering this as an alternative and&nbsp;
I'm taking advantage and you should as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:24.480
I think we'll continue to see a lot more of&nbsp;
that as we go forward. Iot, Internet of Things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:29.400
threats. Yes. In fact, we've seen there was one&nbsp;
study that came out that said there was in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:40.520
a fourth percent increase in Iot attacks in this&nbsp;
year, 2023. So that one is definitely continued.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:45.880
We don't see that one changing. I already talked&nbsp;
about AI That one's only going to get bigger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:51.840
as we would guess. And then quantum computing. I&nbsp;
talked about that one last year, and in particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.680
that quantum systems are going to one day be&nbsp;
able to crack our cryptography. They haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:01.920
effectively done that yet, but we're one year&nbsp;
closer to it. So this is one of those you can say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:05.840
well, it's it's sort of true. We're definitely&nbsp;
closer to the point when that's going to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:11.920
a real threat to us. Not here exactly yet.&nbsp;
One bit of good news. I can report, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:18.280
that I was partly right and partly wrong on, and&nbsp;
that is the skills gap. So the skills gap actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:25.600
moved from what was 770,000 open positions&nbsp;
in the cybersecurity space to now, according&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:32.800
to Cyber Secord. We're down to about 570,000,&nbsp;
so that's an improvement. I predicted that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:37.960
would still have a skills gap, and we do, but it&nbsp;
actually has has gone down a little bit. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:43.400
hope that continues to be the case because we need&nbsp;
a lot of good guys in here who are going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:48.920
able to fight the good fight. Thanks for watching.&nbsp;
If you found this video interesting and would like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:56.880
to learn more about cybersecurity, please remember&nbsp;
to hit like and subscribe to this channel.

